<exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="exercisegroup-orthog-angle-size-R2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Size of angle in <m> \R^2 </m></title>

<introduction><p>
	In each case:
	<ol>
		<li> Use the dot product to determine if the angle between <m> \uvec{v} </m> and <m> \uvec{w} </m> is acute, right, or obtuse. </li>
		<li> Are <m> \uvec{v} </m> and <m> \uvec{w} </m> <term><xref ref="definition-orthog-orthogonal" text="custom">orthogonal</xref></term>? </li>
		<li>
			Draw a diagram of <m> \uvec{v} </m> and <m> \uvec{w} </m> in the <m> xy </m>-plane,
			with initial points of the vectors at the origin.
		</li>
	</ol>
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> \uvec{v} = ( 9,   8) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{w} = (-4, 9/2) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li> Right: <m> \udotprod{v}{w} = 0 </m>. </li>

		<li> Yes. </li>

		<li><image width="43.2%">
			<latex-image label="image-orthog-exercises-orthog-angle-size-R2-right-1">
				<xi:include href="angle-size-R2.d/right-1.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating two orthogonal vectors. </shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				A diagram consisting of two vectors at a right angle in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
				A directed line segment labelled as vector <m> \uvec{v} </m> extends from the origin to the point <m> (9,2) </m>.
				A second directed line segment labelled as vector <m> \uvec{w} </m> extends from the origin to the point <m> (-4,9/2) </m>.
				Where these vectors meet at the origin forms a right angle.
			</p></description>
		</image></li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> \uvec{v} = (3, -2) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{w} = (1,  6) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li>
			Obtuse:
			<m> \udotprod{v}{w} = -3 \lt 0 </m>
			and the vectors are not parallel
			(not scalar multiples of one another)
			so the angle cannot be straight.
		</li>

		<li> No. </li>

		<li><image width="23.5%">
			<latex-image label="image-orthog-exercises-orthog-angle-size-R2-obtuse">
				<xi:include href="angle-size-R2.d/obtuse.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating two vectors at an obtuse angle. </shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				A diagram consisting of two vectors at an obtuse angle in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
				A directed line segment labelled as vector <m> \uvec{v} </m> extends from the origin to the point <m> (3,-2) </m>.
				A second directed line segment labelled as vector <m> \uvec{w} </m> extends from the origin to the point <m> (1,6) </m>.
				Where these vectors meet at the origin forms an obtuse angle.
			</p></description>
		</image></li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> \uvec{v} = (-6, -2) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{w} = (-8,  4) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li>
			Acute:
			<m> \udotprod{v}{w} = 40 \gt 0 </m>
			and the vectors are not parallel
			(not scalar multiples of one another)
			so the angle cannot be zero.
		</li>

		<li> No. </li>

		<li><image width="34.7%">
			<latex-image label="image-orthog-exercises-orthog-angle-size-R2-acute">
				<xi:include href="angle-size-R2.d/acute.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating two vectors at an acute angle. </shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				A diagram consisting of two vectors at an acute angle in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
				A directed line segment labelled as vector <m> \uvec{v} </m> extends from the origin to the point <m> (-6,-2) </m>.
				A second directed line segment labelled as vector <m> \uvec{w} </m> extends from the origin to the point <m> (-8,4) </m>.
				Where these vectors meet at the origin forms an acute angle.
			</p></description>
		</image></li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>
	<statement><p>
		<m> \uvec{v} = ( 5,   2) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{w} = ( 4, -10) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li> Right: <m> \udotprod{v}{w} = 0 </m>. </li>

		<li> Yes. </li>

		<li><image width="32.6%">
			<latex-image label="image-orthog-exercises-orthog-angle-size-R2-right-2">
				<xi:include href="angle-size-R2.d/right-2.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating two orthogonal vectors. </shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				A diagram consisting of two vectors at a right angle in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
				A directed line segment labelled as vector <m> \uvec{v} </m> extends from the origin to the point <m> (5,2) </m>.
				A second directed line segment labelled as vector <m> \uvec{w} </m> extends from the origin to the point <m> (4,-10) </m>.
				Where these vectors meet at the origin forms a right angle.
			</p></description>
		</image></li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
